00:12:06.600 --> 00:12:09.920
there's noises that signify he's changed locations.

00:12:09.920 --> 00:12:12.960
And the 3 locations; one being center, right, and left.

00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:18.860
Uh, if he makes a noise in that department it doesn't mean that he's at the door, it just means that he's moved over to that area.

00:12:18.860 --> 00:12:25.440
It's only if he makes a second noise in that area, that I then shift my attention over to, say the right side right here.

00:12:25.440 --> 00:12:31.060
And it may not seem like like I'm seeing anything, but when I flip up the screen I'm actually gathering data.

00:12:31.060 --> 00:12:34.920
You know I know he's there or I know he got closer, I can see that.

00:12:34.920 --> 00:12:40.900
It comes from my, you know, hours and hours of practice of doing Five Nights at Freddy's 20/20 modes over the years.

00:12:40.900 --> 00:12:48.460
You know, I'm very good at flipping the screen up and just grabbing what little detail I need to spare as little battery as possible.

00:12:48.680 --> 00:12:54.280
And then using majority the auditory cues to know when and where and what to do things

00:12:54.280 --> 00:13:01.860
so if I know that he's moved to one area, I also know that he can come back, but I know that there's no danger unless he makes 2 noises

00:13:01.860 --> 00:13:03.420
in the same field.

00:13:03.420 --> 00:13:09.180
